These two theorems assert the Agoh-Giuga conjecture, which states that a natural number $p \geq 2$ is prime if and only if $p \cdot B_{p-1} \equiv -1 \pmod{p}$ (equivalently, $\sum_{i=1}^{p-1} i^{p-1} \equiv -1 \pmod{p}$). This is an **unsolved conjecture** — no proof or disproof is known. It has been verified computationally for large ranges but remains open.

